* ''past'' noon
* ''on top of'' the fridge
* ''owing to'' the rain
}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|grammar|strict sense}} Any of a class of non-[[inflect]]ing [[word]]s and multiword [[term#English:_name for a concept|terms]] typically employed to connect a following [[noun]] or a [[pronoun]], in an [[adjective|adjectival]] or [[adverb|adverbial]] sense, with some other word: a [[particle]] used with a noun or pronoun (in English always in the [[objective]] case) to make a [[phrase]] limiting some other word.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1844|author=E. A. Andrews|title=First Lessions in Latin; or Introduction to Andrews and Stoddard's Latin Grammar|edition=6th|page=91|location=Boston
|passage=322. The parts of speech which are neither declined nor conjugated, are called by the general name of particles. 323. They are adverbs, '''prepositions''', conjunctions, and interjections.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=Andrew Radford|title=Transformational grammar: a first course|location=Cambridge, UK|publisher=Cambridge University Press|page=495|chapter=9|passage=And in (121) below, we see that when a wh-NP is used as the Object of a '''Preposition''', the whole Prepositional Phrase can undergo WH <small>MOVEMENT</small>:<br> (121) (a) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; [''To whom''] can I send this letter —?<br> <span style="color:white;">(121)</span> (b) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; [''About what''] are they quarrelling —?<br> <span style="color:white;">(121)</span> (c) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; [''In which book''] did you read about it —?}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=Jun 1 2014|actor=w:John Oliver|title=w:Last Week Tonight with John Oliver|season=1|number=5|episode=Net Neutrality|network=HBO|text=I love this girl. “On which I can get my hands” — even in her darkest moment, she cannot bring herself to end a sentence with a '''preposition'''.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar|broad sense}} An [[adposition]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[proposition]]; an [[exposition]]; a [[discourse]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1811|author=w:Robert Fabyan|title=The New Chronicles of England and France|url=https://archive.org/details/cu31924027941529|page=116|text={{...}} he made a longe '''preposicion''' & oracion cōcernynge  yͤ allegiaūce which he exortyd his lordes to owe|editor=Sir Henry Ellis|origyear=1516}}

